WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.000
This tutorial shows how to use the Sock-Pilot
Policy Workbench.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.300
A response proposal can contain useful
actions and still violate

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.700
an explicit policy. We will inspect a
proposal, check it, examine

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.400
the repair, and export an auditable result.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.000
Everything here uses synthetic input.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.900
The workbench executes no response actions on
a

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.600
Extract the anonymous zip linked in the
paper.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.400
With Python 3.12 available, run bash
reproduce.sh workbench.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.500
First, use downloads pinned dependencies and
qualifies an isolated

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.800
installation. Later runs verify and reuse it.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.900
Open the local address printed in the
terminal.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.500
No model key is needed.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.800
The guided example makes the policy visible
before we check anything.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.100
Forensic evidence must be collected before
re-imaging, and isolation

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.700
is prohibited in this example.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.400
The proposal re-images the host and then
isolates it.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.200
Select Check and Repair. Two rules are
violated.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.500
The engine removes isolation and inserts
forensic collection before

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.100
re-imaging. The resulting sequence has no
remaining violations

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.100
under these declared rules. This is a policy
verdict, not evidence

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.000
that an action succeeded in an incident.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.700
An action outside the catalog is rejected.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.600
Notice that editing the input clears the old
verdict and disables

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.400
its export. You cannot accidentally download
the result for a different

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.200
proposal. Load the compliant example and
check again.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.000
It needs no policy repair.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.000
Validation failures are visible, and recovery
does not require

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.100
restarting the application.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.600
Now switch to your structured policy case and
import the supplied

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.200
service recovery example. This is a different
action catalog.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.200
Its proposal restarts a service and
quarantines an account.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.800
The declared rules require memory capture
before restart and approval

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.300
for quarantine. No approval is supplied.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.600
Check the case. The repair inserts memory
capture and removes the

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.900
unapproved action. Approval fields are
declarations in the input,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.600
not authenticated authorization. You can
inspect the complete case

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.600
and the rule results before downloading
anything.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.200
Download the normalized input case and the
result.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.300
The result retains the proposed sequence, the
rules, the edits,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.400
the repaired sequence, and the verdicts
before and after repair.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.600
Its input hash identifies the case that was
checked.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.200
Keep the files together with the package
version.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.800
These records let another researcher inspect
how a declared policy

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.900
affected this particular proposal. The

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.900
The command line uses the same checker and
repair service.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.200
Pass the downloaded input to the case input
option and save the

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.400
JSON output. Compare the complete output with
the file exported

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.400
by the interface. They must match.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.800
The command shown here performs that
comparison on the actual files.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.000
It does not replace the checker with a
separate implementation.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.000
A

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.200
A stable repair is not necessarily a
compliant plan.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.800
This final synthetic case contains
conflicting rules.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.700
Memory capture is mandatory and prohibited.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.800
The workbench leaves the mandatory rule
violated, even though a

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.100
second repair leaves the sequence unchanged.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.400
Read remaining violations as well as
stability.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.300
The tool does not turn conflicting
requirements into an approval.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.900
To reproduce the paper's preserved results,
run bash reproduce.sh

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.700
all in this same artifact.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.400
These synthetic examples demonstrate policy
checking, not incident

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.600
response execution.
